SUMMARY: The diagnosis and the fix are right. I reproduced the bug empirically: with the HEAD version of /workspace/src/chart/pie/labelLayout.ts, a 41-slice pie (one dominant + 40 tiny, startAngle 180, 500x400) produces `dx = NaN` in `recalculateXOnSemiToAlignOnEllipseCurve`, which flows `newX -> item.label.x`, and the final loop at labelLayout.ts:556 (`const notShowLabel = isNaN(label.x) || isNaN(label.y)`) then sets `label.ignore = true` plus `each(label.states, setNotShow)` and `labelLine.ignore = true` — three labels ("Tiny 0/1/2") were silently dropped. With the clamp applied, all 41 labels are kept and no NaN remains. NaN dx is indeed the only unintended drop mechanism inside this file; the other drop sites (`minShowLabelAngle` at :400, `hideOverlap` via PieSeries `labelLayout: {hideOverlap: true}`) are intentional, and `setNotShow`/`isPositionCenter` have no defect. I confirmed the removal of `Math.abs(...)` is a pure no-op: `dy*dy >= 0` and `rB2 = rB*rB >= 0` (rB is always a `Math.sqrt` result or `rA`), so the quotient is never negative; `Math.max(1 - t, 0)` is also algebraically identical to upstream's `1 - Math.min(1, t)` form. There is no behavior change at all for inputs whose radicand was already >= 0, so other overlap-avoidance is untouched, and no duplicate/stray labels are introduced. Root cause detail worth recording: the negative radicand comes solely from the `rB` fallback in `recalculateX` (labelLayout.ts:125-127) — when the max-dy label satisfies `Math.abs(dx) >= rA` (which happens for any positive `distanceToLabelLine` on a label near the horizontal axis, since `dx = nx*rA + labelDistance`), `rB` is set to `rA` instead of something >= `maxY`, so every item with `dy > rA` goes negative. In the sqrt branch `rB >= maxY` always holds, so clamping can never trigger there. Two minor quality/robustness gaps remain (see issues); neither drops a label, so I consider the change correct and complete for the stated requirement.
  [minor] src/chart/pie/labelLayout.ts:125
    PROBLEM: Clamping the radicand to 0 fixes the NaN but yields dx = 0, i.e. newX = cx + len2*dir, which yanks the affected labels horizontally back to the pie's centre line and collapses several of them onto the exact same x. The real defect is the `rB` fallback `: rA` in recalculateX, which can be smaller than `maxY`; making the fallback >= maxY removes the negative radicand at its source and keeps the ellipse curve monotonic.
    SCENARIO: Verified with the repo's own new test case (one 1000-value slice + 40 value-1 slices, radius '50%', startAngle 180, 500x400 canvas, cx=250 cy=200 r=100, labelLine length 15 / length2 15, distanceToLabelLine 5): the bottom-most left label has |dx| = rA + 5 = 120 > rA = 115, so rB = rA = 115, and the three labels with dy = 121.2 / 118.1 / 115.0 all clamp to dx = 0 and land on the identical x = 235 (= cx - len2), while their neighbour 'Tiny 3' sits at x = 208.7 — the curve reverses direction. Worse, the guide line of 'Tiny 0' then runs from the sector edge (153,224) to (240,321); its direction (87,97) has a negative dot product with the outward radial (-97,24), and its midpoint (196.5,272.5) is 90.1 px from cx/cy (< r = 100), i.e. the label line is drawn straight across the pie body. With rB = Math.max(rA, dy) instead, 'Tiny 1' would get dx = 115*sqrt(1-(118.1/121.2)^2) = 25.8 -> x = 209.2, smoothly continuing the curve next to 'Tiny 3' at 208.7, and only the single extreme label would degenerate to dx = 0.
    FIX: In `recalculateX`, widen the degenerate fallback so rB always covers maxY, keeping the clamp as a belt-and-braces guard:

                const rB = Math.abs(dx) < rA
                    ? Math.sqrt(dy * dy / (1 - dx * dx / rA / rA))
                    : Math.max(rA, dy);
