line-memory-reduction :: correct
SUMMARY: The rewrite is behaviorally equivalent for everything that affects appearance. I verified the new `turnPointsIntoStep` against a verbatim copy of the HEAD algorithm over 96 combinations (pointCount 0/1/2/3/5, nulls leading/interior/trailing/all-null, stepTurnAt start|middle|end, connectNulls true|false, base axis = x and = y) by driving real charts and comparing `polyline.shape.points` element-by-element: identical length, order and values (within float32 rounding), and identical NaN placement. Counts confirmed: start/end = 2n-1, middle = 3n-2 (3*(n-1)+1), 0 for n=0; `pointCount === 0` and `points.length === 0` produce an empty array as before, and `stackedOnPoints == null` is handled. Both output arrays are written from the same `outOffset` and `writeStepPoints` advances the offset purely as a function of `stepTurnAt` and `nextSrcOffset < 0`, so points/stackedOnPoints cannot desync (step.test.ts's base-coord alignment assertions pass). Every slot of the exactly-sized array is written (4n-2 / 6n-4 writes), so the no-Float32Array fallback (`new Array(n)`) leaves no holes. Float32 is safe: layout points are already float32, only `middle` is re-rounded (sub-pixel), and `isPointsSame`/`_doUpdateAnimation` diffing operate on the raw layout points, not step points. No consumer push()es or resizes `shape.points` (poly.ts, lineAnimationDiff.ts, LineView's during-callback all only read/index); zrender's `fillArray` early-returns on typed arrays but keyframe values are plain-array clones (`cloneValue` → `Array.prototype.slice.call`), so length alignment and NaN repair still happen — no crash. Every `this._points`/`this._stackedOnPoints` read is null-safe: `!canDiffPoints` short-circuits before `isPointsSame`, `_doUpdateAnimation` (the only reader of `_stackedOnPoints`) is gated by `canDiffPoints`, and `_initOrUpdateEndLabel`/`_endLabelOnDuring`/`highlight` use `data.getLayout('points')` instead. The reused `stackedData` buffer is safe: neither `Cartesian2D.dataToPoint` (writes into a fresh `out`) nor `Polar.dataToPoint` retains the input, both indices 0 and 1 are always assigned, and length stays 2. `npx tsc --noEmit` clean; line/step/memory tests and the rest of the ut suite pass. The memory win is real for the step path (exactly-sized float32 instead of growing double arrays, plus no intermediate `filteredPoints` copies), though the `_points = null` half of the change frees nothing (see issues).
