  [minor] src/chart/pie/labelLayout.ts:125
    PROBLEM: Clamping the radicand to 0 fixes the NaN but yields dx = 0, i.e. newX = cx + len2*dir, which yanks the affected labels horizontally back to the pie's centre line and collapses several of them onto the exact same x. The real defect is the `rB` fallback `: rA` in recalculateX, which can be smaller than `maxY`; making the fallback >= maxY removes the negative radicand at its source and keeps the ellipse curve monotonic.
    SCENARIO: Verified with the repo's own new test case (one 1000-value slice + 40 value-1 slices, radius '50%', startAngle 180, 500x400 canvas, cx=250 cy=200 r=100, labelLine length 15 / length2 15, distanceToLabelLine 5): the bottom-most left label has |dx| = rA + 5 = 120 > rA = 115, so rB = rA = 115, and the three labels with dy = 121.2 / 118.1 / 115.0 all clamp to dx = 0 and land on the identical x = 235 (= cx - len2), while their neighbour 'Tiny 3' sits at x = 208.7 — the curve reverses direction. Worse, the guide line of 'Tiny 0' then runs from the sector edge (153,224) to (240,321); its direction (87,97) has a negative dot product with the outward radial (-97,24), and its midpoint (196.5,272.5) is 90.1 px from cx/cy (< r = 100), i.e. the label line is drawn straight across the pie body. With rB = Math.max(rA, dy) instead, 'Tiny 1' would get dx = 115*sqrt(1-(118.1/121.2)^2) = 25.8 -> x = 209.2, smoothly continuing the curve next to 'Tiny 3' at 208.7, and only the single extreme label would degenerate to dx = 0.
    FIX: In `recalculateX`, widen the degenerate fallback so rB always covers maxY, keeping the clamp as a belt-and-braces guard:

                const rB = Math.abs(dx) < rA
                    ? Math.sqrt(dy * dy / (1 - dx * dx / rA / rA))
                    : Math.max(rA, dy);
  [minor] src/chart/pie/labelLayout.ts:97
    PROBLEM: The clamp does not close the NaN hole completely: `Math.max(NaN, 0)` is NaN, so when rB2 === 0 and dy === 0 the expression `dy * dy / rB2` is 0/0 = NaN and dx is still NaN, which still drops the label at line 556. This is pre-existing (HEAD computed `1 - Math.abs(NaN)` = NaN too), but the comment added by the change claims the clamp keeps dx from being NaN, and the requirement is that labels which should be shown are never dropped.
    SCENARIO: rB === 0 requires the max-dy item of a semi to have dy === 0 with |dx| < rA. dx = nx*rA + sign(nx)*labelDistance, so |dx| < rA at ny === 0 (nx = +/-1) needs labelDistance < 0. Concretely: a pie with `label.distanceToLabelLine: -10` where exactly one slice is centred on angle 0 (so label.y === cy exactly, e.g. a 50%-cumulative-midpoint slice) and every other label on that side lies below cy, with shiftLayoutOnY returning true. Then topSemi = [that one item], rB = sqrt(0 / (1 - dx^2/rA^2)) = 0, rB2 = 0, dy = 0, dy*dy/rB2 = NaN, Math.max(NaN, 0) = NaN, dx = NaN -> label.x = NaN -> label.ignore = true and labelLine.ignore = true. Contrived but a real drop path, and the same guard also protects against any future NaN reaching newX.
    FIX: Guard the degenerate ratio (and/or the final value) explicitly:

            // Use ellipse implicit function to calculate x
            const ratio = rB2 > 0 ? Math.min(1, dy * dy / rB2) : 1;
            const dx = Math.sqrt((1 - ratio) * rA2);
            const newX = cx + (dx + item.len2) * dir;

or, more defensively, `const newX = isNaN(dx) ? item.label.x : cx + (dx + item.len2) * dir;` so an unexpected NaN leaves the label at its current x rather than hiding it.
