line-memory-reduction :: correct
  [minor] src/chart/line/LineView.ts:1009
    PROBLEM: Releasing `_points` when `!hasAnimation` frees no memory — `points` is `data.getLayout('points')`, i.e. the same array the SeriesData already owns (the new test at test/ut/spec/chart/line/memory.test.ts:86 asserts exactly this identity). The only genuine saving is `_stackedOnPoints` (area charts only). Meanwhile nulling `_points` permanently disables the `isPointsSame` short-circuit (`canDiffPoints === false` forces the else branch on every subsequent render), so with `animation: false` every render now rebuilds the step arrays and calls `polyline.setShape`/`polygon.setShape` even when the layout points are byte-identical — extra allocation and shape-dirtying in exactly the configuration this change targets.
    SCENARIO: `animation: false`, `step: 'start'`, category x axis; a re-render that does not change the layout points (legend toggle of another series, a dataZoom move that keeps the same category window, or `setOption` with the same data). Before: `isPointsSame` matched and nothing was re-computed. After: `preparePolyPoints` allocates two fresh Float32Arrays and both shapes are marked dirty on every such render.
    FIX: Keep `_points` (it is a free alias of the data layout) and release only the extra buffer, keeping the diff usable for the common non-area case:

```ts
const canDiffStackedOn = !stackedOnPoints || !!this._stackedOnPoints;
if (!this._points
    || !isPointsSame(this._points, points)
    || !canDiffStackedOn
    || !isPointsSame(this._stackedOnPoints, stackedOnPoints)
) { ... }
...
this._points = points;
this._stackedOnPoints = hasAnimation ? stackedOnPoints : null;
```
  [minor] src/chart/line/LineView.ts:212
    PROBLEM: `getStackedOnPoints` returns a plain `[]` (truthy, length 0) when `!dataCoordInfo.valueDim`. HEAD then produced an empty stepped `stackedOnPoints` (`toStepPoints([])` → `[]`); the new code sizes `stepStackedOnPoints` from `points` instead and fills it by reading out-of-range indices of the empty source, yielding a full-length all-NaN array rather than an empty one.
    SCENARIO: An area line series whose value dimension cannot be mapped (`dataCoordInfo.valueDim` falsy) plus `step`: `polygon.shape.stackedOnPoints` is now `Float32Array(2*(2n-1))` of NaN where it used to be length 0. Rendering is unaffected (`ECPolygon.buildPath`'s `isPointNull` treats NaN and `undefined` identically, so the base edge is skipped either way), but it is an output divergence and a wasted allocation on a path whose whole point is saving memory.
    FIX: Treat an empty source as absent: `const hasStackedOn = !!(stackedOnPoints && stackedOnPoints.length);` (or make `getStackedOnPoints` return `null` instead of `[]` when `!dataCoordInfo.valueDim`).
  [minor] src/chart/line/LineView.ts:211
    PROBLEM: In the animated step path (`_doUpdateAnimation`), `polyline.shape.points` is now a fixed-size Float32Array, so zrender's array interpolation can no longer grow it. `Track.step` writes `out[i]` for `i < frame.value.length` into `target[propName]`; when the target keyframe is longer than the current one, `fillArray` pads the *keyframe* (a plain clone) but the writes past the end of the Float32Array target are silently dropped, leaving the shape truncated for the rest of the transition. I demonstrated the mechanism directly (ECPolyline animating 3→5 points: plain array target ends at length 10, Float32Array target ends at length 6).
    SCENARIO: Requires `steppedNext.points.length > steppedCurrent.points.length`, which needs `connectNulls: true` plus NaN in `diff.current` at an index where `diff.next` is finite. I could not construct this through `lineAnimationDiff` (the '=' branch copies the next point over a NaN current point, and the '+' branch computes the current point from valid new-data values), and all realistic transitions I exercised (same length, more data, less data, null appearing, null disappearing, more data with nulls, degenerate previous value axis) ended at exactly the points a non-animated render produces. So this is a latent risk rather than an observed regression.
    FIX: Make the animation source at least as long as the target in `_doUpdateAnimation`, e.g. right before `polyline.shape.points = current`:

```ts
if (current.length < next.length) {
    const padded = createFloat32Array(next.length);
    for (let i = 0; i < current.length; i++) { padded[i] = current[i]; }
    for (let i = current.length; i < next.length; i++) { padded[i] = next[i]; }
    current = padded;
}
```
(and the same for `stackedOnCurrent` vs `stackedOnNext`).
